The .lif as a one-time folder (user)
------------------------------------
Tried: "the trapped directory recursion occurs regardless ... it changes
into this [sub] folder but then BACK INTO THE LIF. i think the lif
should be treated not as a regular, but as a one-time folder. left
gesture does not go into its own folder, but a level up, as if it was
just a file. same for right gesture ... it needs to be opened, by
scrolling onto it, opening it from the side panel or from an external
source". And: the window disappeared at the right click only when Image
Lab started with a .lif as the last image (it had saved the .lif as
LastDirectory; side by side then sent Total Commander there).
- A container is no node of the tree any more. Its images (virtual
  names) are listed among its folder's own images, at its place (by
  name or date: the container's path and time sort them together):
  the wheel walks into it and out; folder gestures pass it.
- ItemDirectory of an image inside a container = the folder it lies in.
- Opening a container (drop, TC, the panel, the command line): its
  folder, on its first image (or on the image named to resume on).
- Removed: container nodes (ScanContainer, IsContainer), a container's
  own listing (ListContainersOf, AssignContainers). New:
  AddContainerEntries, FirstOfContainer.
- LastDirectory is now always a real folder (the navigator's root).
- TestNavigation's container part rewritten (the tree has no container
  node, the folder lists 4 images, the wheel walks in, next folder
  passes it, opening the container lands on its first image, the root
  is the folder's parent as for a file).
- Review (independent): no compile errors, MView unchanged, all checks
  traced as passing. Taken: resume on the named image when an older ini
  names the container as the folder; stale comments.
- Cost noted: the start listing now reads the description of every .lif
  in the folder (a few ms each).

